      <section id="physics" class="section">
        <h2>The Physics of Capillary Action Lubrication</h2>
        <p>The ability of a penetrating oil to climb upward into tight threads against gravity is governed by the physics of a capillary action lubricant. This behavior is mathematically modeled by the Washburn equation, which states that the rate of penetration is directly proportional to the liquid's surface tension and the cosine of the contact angle, and inversely proportional to its dynamic viscosity. Therefore, to maximize wicking speed into sub-micron gaps, the formulation must combine an extremely low viscosity with a contact angle that approaches zero on rusted steel. To study how these fluid properties are tested during laboratory scale-up, refer to our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/from-lab-to-commercial-launch" target="_top">moving formulations from lab to factory</a>.</p>
        <p>When a penetrating spray is applied to a seized bolt, the liquid forms a meniscus at the entrance of the thread clearance. If the contact angle is small (indicating high wetting behavior), the capillary pressure is positive, drawing the liquid inward. The wicking rate is critical; if the fluid is too viscous, the time required to travel the length of the thread exceeds practical maintenance limits. Naphthenic base oils with low pour points and light aliphatic solvents ensure the viscosity remains below 5 centistokes (cSt) at room temperature, facilitating rapid wicking through tight, thread-like channels.</p>

