      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="solvency" class="section">
        <h2>Solvent Systems and Surface Tension Lowering</h2>
        <p>Lowering the surface tension of a penetrant spray chemistry is critical to achieving the wetting speeds required for heavy-duty industrial applications. Pure mineral oils have surface tensions around 30 to 32 mN/m, which prevents them from wicking into micro-fractures. In contrast, modern penetrating formulations use specialized solvent systems to lower the surface tension below 24 mN/m. The thermodynamic properties of these organic solvents can be cross-referenced in the peer-reviewed chemical database <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem</a>, which catalogs chemical structures, physical properties, and safety indices.</p>
        <p>By blending low-surface-tension aliphatic fractions with highly polar solvents, formulation chemists can optimize both wetting speed and solvency. The aliphatic solvents act as highly mobile carriers that spread the mixture across the substrate. Meanwhile, the polar solvents target polar corrosion components, wicking through the rust to reach the underlying metal. This combination ensures that the active lubricant is carried directly to the seized interface, where it can perform its primary friction-reduction role.</p>

